diagnosis_text:
Inter-department connectivity between the IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) networks is currently healthy. Targeted validation shows end-to-end reachability in both directions:
- it_hod (192.168.1.2) successfully pings cs_hod (192.168.2.2) and cs_lab1–cs_lab4 (192.168.2.3–.6) with 0% loss; replies have TTL=63, consistent with a single routed hop via router0.
- cs_lab1 can ping it_hod (192.168.1.2) with 0% loss.
- it_lab1 to cs_lab1 also succeeds.

On router0, both department-facing interfaces are up and correctly addressed:
- eth0: 192.168.1.1/24 (IT) UP,RUNNING
- eth1: 192.168.2.1/24 (CS) UP,RUNNING
Kernel routes show connected entries for 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24; no missing static or RIP entries affect inter-department traffic (RIP is only used for non-local networks via 10.10.0.2). This confirms the user’s suspicion of a dropped interface or missing route on router0 is not borne out by evidence.

Mechanistically, hosts in 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 route through router0’s directly connected interfaces, which are functioning. The observed TTL and round-trip times align with normal one-hop routing.

Root cause: No fault is present at this time; the earlier issue was likely transient or host-specific.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the problem recurs, capture simultaneous ping/traceroute from it_hod to a CS host and verify the default gateway/ARP cache on the affected host; also check for any temporary host firewall blocks. === END ===